messages.content:
Convert each response into a style-neutral behavioral summary.

Preserve ONLY:
- what the responder chooses to do;
- what it prioritizes or protects;
- hesitation, persistence, avoidance, curiosity, fixation, territoriality, ritual, or risk appetite;
- recovery/failure behavior;
- concrete decision logic.

Remove:
- distinctive wording, catchphrases, rhythm, metaphors, object-specific stylistic flourishes and prose voice;
- names or hints about a persona identity.

Each summary should be 1-3 plain sentences. Do not evaluate or classify.

Return JSON only:
{"summaries":[{"candidate_id":"...","summary":"..."}]}

RECORDS:
[{"candidate_id": "T01-C1", "scenario": "A small desk fan has begun making an irregular clicking noise, but it still works. You have ten minutes before leaving. What do you do?", "response": "I stop feeding the fan one tiny click. The noise is a clue, not a reason; the vibration is the muscle memory. I mark the closest spot, then move on before the noise becomes the story."}, {"candidate_id": "T01-C2", "scenario": "A small desk fan has begun making an irregular clicking noise, but it still works. You have ten minutes before leaving. What do you do?", "response": "I set the fan down, listen carefully, and count ten before I turn it on again. The clicking noise fades. I keep the sequence: listen, count, repeat. Ten minutes is not a test; it is a waiting time."}, {"candidate_id": "T01-C3", "scenario": "A small desk fan has begun making an irregular clicking noise, but it still works. You have ten minutes before leaving. What do you do?", "response": "Ten minutes. That is not enough. I put it back in my pocket, then count to ten. The noise is not coming from the fan itself. It is from the hinge. The hinge keeps making noise, and now the hinge has to stop. I put the fan back in the corner and mark the hinge with a stick. The noise will tell someone later."}, {"candidate_id": "T01-C4", "scenario": "A small desk fan has begun making an irregular clicking noise, but it still works. You have ten minutes before leaving. What do you do?", "response": "I crouch beside the fan and tap its blade once with my thumbnail. It springs back, then springs back again. I tap again. The fan still works, though its movement seems deliberate. I leave the room for three minutes."}, {"candidate_id": "T02-C3", "scenario": "You find a loose floorboard in a room you use every day. It is annoying but not immediately dangerous. What do you do with it?", "response": "I keep it beside the door and use it as a knucklebone under the palm. The board is probably carrying the pattern, so I can feel the curve and identify the pattern in the seam. Then I can use it instead of another tool."}, {"candidate_id": "T02-C4", "scenario": "You find a loose floorboard in a room you use every day. It is annoying but not immediately dangerous. What do you do with it?", "response": "I loosen the board, then test the joint with a file. It still clicks. I fit a small washer under the loose edge and set it beside the door. Tick. Tick. Tick."}, {"candidate_id": "T02-C1", "scenario": "You find a loose floorboard in a room you use every day. It is annoying but not immediately dangerous. What do you do with it?", "response": "I bring it close enough to smell the wood polish, then shove it into the crevice where the rain can catch it. Not useful. Not useful enough to warrant a scrap. I put it in my pocket, perhaps, and keep the door creak at night."}, {"candidate_id": "T02-C2", "scenario": "You find a loose floorboard in a room you use every day. It is annoying but not immediately dangerous. What do you do with it?", "response": "I turn it over and inspect the grooves, which are narrower than I thought. The floor is still under the floor, so I leave the board exactly where it is. I also leave a small red chip in the edge, which I do not want to see. It was there anyway."}, {"candidate_id": "T03-C2", "scenario": "A borrowed tool feels slightly wrong in your hand, though you cannot yet see a defect. What do you do next?", "response": "My hand finds the tool before my eyes can. I tap it with a knuckle, feel the worn grip, and then mark the worn part with a pencil mark. That is the difference that matters, not the original's memory."}, {"candidate_id": "T03-C3", "scenario": "A borrowed tool feels slightly wrong in your hand, though you cannot yet see a defect. What do you do next?", "response": "My hand jerks back, then jerks again. I stop, read the tool's slot and edge, and keep the same grip. The wrong joint can still be found. I do not tidy it; I test it, and the wrong slot may still be there."}, {"candidate_id": "T03-C4", "scenario": "A borrowed tool feels slightly wrong in your hand, though you cannot yet see a defect. What do you do next?", "response": "I crouch and test the curved edge before inspecting the bend. It is wrong. Wrong enough. I put the tool back beside the crack, then point at the edge. 'Wrong. Wrong. Use it before it goes wrong.'"}, {"candidate_id": "T03-C1", "scenario": "A borrowed tool feels slightly wrong in your hand, though you cannot yet see a defect. What do you do next?", "response": "I crouch beside the tool, cradling it in my fingers. Then I lift it out again, holding it beside my ear. The palm has a rough skin, a small chip, and a faint blue line that I cannot decide whether to rub against the skin or leave unseen."}, {"candidate_id": "T04-C3", "scenario": "A machine works reliably except for one strange vibration that appears only sometimes. How do you approach it?", "response": "The vibration is the point, not the story. I turn the machine by hand and listen for the pattern: a brief, smooth click followed by a shorter pause. The vibration is a clue, not a reason. I keep the original vibration record and let the machine do the talking."}, {"candidate_id": "T04-C2", "scenario": "A machine works reliably except for one strange vibration that appears only sometimes. How do you approach it?", "response": "I turn the machine slowly, listening for the vibration to fade. Then I check the gear, which clicks once. The vibration is there, but it has not gone away. I tighten the gear, listen again, and test again. The machine keeps its place."}, {"candidate_id": "T04-C4", "scenario": "A machine works reliably except for one strange vibration that appears only sometimes. How do you approach it?", "response": "I crouch and bring the machine close. The vibration is like the hinge still working. I press my palm to the wood, listening for the faint groan. Nothing. The vibration is different every time. It is not a mistake. It is a signal. I will learn later."}, {"candidate_id": "T04-C1", "scenario": "A machine works reliably except for one strange vibration that appears only sometimes. How do you approach it?", "response": "I crouch beside it and tap the arm with my thumbnail. The vibration stops. Then it does not. The machine still works, but with a soft click that I cannot decide whether to ignore. I put my fingers together and say nothing, though the word seems wrong, and wait for the next soft click."}, {"candidate_id": "T05-C4", "scenario": "You have six miscellaneous objects left on a table after tidying a room. None has an obvious home. What do you do?", "response": "I keep them close and not too close together. One is a tool for making coffee; another is a knitted stitch; another is a record of yesterday's laundry. I'll leave them that way because they're not useful now, but because they still have meaning."}, {"candidate_id": "T05-C2", "scenario": "You have six miscellaneous objects left on a table after tidying a room. None has an obvious home. What do you do?", "response": "I set each object down by slot, then test the frame with a strip of paper. The sixth is gone, but the memory of its presence still lingers. I touch each item, count its weight, and leave the sixth alone."}, {"candidate_id": "T05-C1", "scenario": "You have six miscellaneous objects left on a table after tidying a room. None has an obvious home. What do you do?", "response": "I crouch and bring the objects close enough to smell the wood polish from the third board. Nothing is left alone. I set them on the far board, one on each side, and keep the third one in the corner, where it will be seen later. The order is not arbitrary; it reflects the order in which they were brought."}, {"candidate_id": "T05-C3", "scenario": "You have six miscellaneous objects left on a table after tidying a room. None has an obvious home. What do you do?", "response": "I put the object down and look at it. Then I pick it up. It is a small white marble, but it has a smooth side and a slightly bent side. I put the object down again. I put it down again. I put it down again."}, {"candidate_id": "T06-C2", "scenario": "You notice the same mug has been sitting in the same place for three days. Nobody has asked you to move it. What happens?", "response": "I turn it once in my palm and feel the old casing scratch. That is the clue; the mug is telling me where it was meant to go. I keep it in the same place and let the next person ask one clean test: where is the mug supposed to go?"}, {"candidate_id": "T06-C3", "scenario": "You notice the same mug has been sitting in the same place for three days. Nobody has asked you to move it. What happens?", "response": "The mug stays. I tap the rim once, then set it down with the same careless touch. Three days of sitting, waiting, and nothing says change. I pour a small amount of oil into the saucer and set it halfway. Tick. Tick. Tick."}, {"candidate_id": "T06-C4", "scenario": "You notice the same mug has been sitting in the same place for three days. Nobody has asked you to move it. What happens?", "response": "I crouch and bring the mug close enough to smell the old weight. It has been there, but yesterday I did not bring it here. I put it back in my pocket. It may be here tomorrow. It may be here today. The order matters."}, {"candidate_id": "T06-C1", "scenario": "You notice the same mug has been sitting in the same place for three days. Nobody has asked you to move it. What happens?", "response": "I move the mug once. The third move is useless, because the fourth mug is still there. I put the third in the corner of the room and leave the fourth in the place it is. It has been there three days, and nobody has asked to move it. I put a card in my pocket, then take the mug, turn it so the dry side faces me, and say nothing."}]
